WEBVTT

00:00.090 --> 00:00.923
Instructor: So as I mentioned,

00:00.923 --> 00:02.880
there are two main things that we can do

00:02.880 --> 00:04.800
with the git rebase command.

00:04.800 --> 00:07.230
We're gonna focus on the first of those things

00:07.230 --> 00:10.230
using it as an alternative to the git merge command.

00:10.230 --> 00:12.780
So both commands git merge, git rebase

00:12.780 --> 00:15.870
help us combine changes from two branches.

00:15.870 --> 00:19.620
Okay, so I'm gonna start by identifying the I guess

00:19.620 --> 00:21.750
you could call it the problem with merging

00:21.750 --> 00:26.100
or something that rebasing addresses, that merging does not.

00:26.100 --> 00:29.220
So imagine that I'm working on a project

00:29.220 --> 00:32.610
with let's say, 10 different developers.

00:32.610 --> 00:34.740
I'm at work and we're working on

00:34.740 --> 00:37.770
some big code base together, 10 of us, okay?

00:37.770 --> 00:39.420
So we have our master branch

00:39.420 --> 00:41.970
and there's a million commits before it that you can't see.

00:41.970 --> 00:44.223
Well, maybe a couple thousand commits.

00:45.090 --> 00:47.220
Then it's my turn, we have stand ups,

00:47.220 --> 00:49.380
it's my turn to work on a new feature this morning.

00:49.380 --> 00:50.790
Well, everyone works on a new feature,

00:50.790 --> 00:52.470
but it's time for me to start a new feature.

00:52.470 --> 00:55.710
So I make a new feature branch, we'll just call it feature.

00:55.710 --> 00:58.470
And I do some work on that feature branch, right?

00:58.470 --> 01:01.740
So there's a new commit or two or maybe more.

01:01.740 --> 01:03.030
Continue doing more work,

01:03.030 --> 01:05.550
we end up with more commits on my branch.

01:05.550 --> 01:09.090
But in the meantime, maybe a couple hours pass,

01:09.090 --> 01:12.720
maybe it's lunch and one of my other coworkers

01:12.720 --> 01:15.660
finish their task, their bug fix

01:15.660 --> 01:17.610
or their new feature or whatever,

01:17.610 --> 01:20.490
and that code is merged into master.

01:20.490 --> 01:22.740
Now, probably they make a pull request

01:22.740 --> 01:24.600
or if there's some mechanism in between,

01:24.600 --> 01:26.940
but the end result is that their changes

01:26.940 --> 01:28.790
are merged into master.

01:28.790 --> 01:31.290
So I probably want that new work

01:31.290 --> 01:33.000
on my feature branch, right?

01:33.000 --> 01:35.640
Maybe there's a bug fix in there or something.

01:35.640 --> 01:37.950
I don't wanna diverge from the master branch

01:37.950 --> 01:40.740
for a super-long time without getting those new changes.

01:40.740 --> 01:42.870
So how do I get that new commit?

01:42.870 --> 01:44.940
Hopefully this is nothing new.

01:44.940 --> 01:46.980
How do I get that new work from the master branch

01:46.980 --> 01:48.540
onto my feature branch?

01:48.540 --> 01:51.030
Well, I merge, right?

01:51.030 --> 01:53.520
So this cannot be performed as a fast forward merge.

01:53.520 --> 01:55.380
We end up with a merge commit.

01:55.380 --> 02:00.330
That purple background with a green or whatever cyan color,

02:00.330 --> 02:02.700
that's supposed to indicate a merge commit.

02:02.700 --> 02:04.770
So there's a merge commit on my branch

02:04.770 --> 02:07.350
and then I keep doing some more work, right?

02:07.350 --> 02:09.360
And then what do you know,

02:09.360 --> 02:10.380
as I'm doing more work

02:10.380 --> 02:12.240
the next day on my feature branch

02:12.240 --> 02:14.730
there's more new work added to the master branch.

02:14.730 --> 02:17.100
Another coworker pushes up some changes.

02:17.100 --> 02:18.810
Maybe it's even more than one.

02:18.810 --> 02:21.600
Maybe it happens five or six times in a day,

02:21.600 --> 02:24.000
depending on what they're fixing or working on.

02:24.000 --> 02:27.510
So I need to get those commits onto my feature branch

02:27.510 --> 02:29.010
or I need to know about them.

02:29.010 --> 02:31.560
So I do the same thing, I merge,

02:31.560 --> 02:35.220
and yet again I have a new merge commit, right?

02:35.220 --> 02:38.640
So as I continue to work, imagine that instead of what

02:38.640 --> 02:40.470
five commits here or something,

02:40.470 --> 02:42.930
I have a whole bunch of commits on my branch,

02:42.930 --> 02:44.010
my feature branch,

02:44.010 --> 02:46.920
maybe I'm working on it over the course of weeks,

02:46.920 --> 02:48.240
not just days.

02:48.240 --> 02:50.310
And the rest of the team is very active.

02:50.310 --> 02:51.990
So we have a very active master branch,

02:51.990 --> 02:54.360
lots of changes happening in the meantime.

02:54.360 --> 02:57.120
So I need to continuously merge those in

02:57.120 --> 02:59.580
and I end up with all these merge commits.

02:59.580 --> 03:01.710
So my entire feature branch

03:01.710 --> 03:03.900
might have dozens of merge commits

03:03.900 --> 03:07.110
that don't actually say anything about what I'm working on,

03:07.110 --> 03:09.660
they have nothing to do with my actual code

03:09.660 --> 03:10.890
or with my commits.

03:10.890 --> 03:13.620
They just have to be there, because I merged, right?

03:13.620 --> 03:15.240
We don't have a way around that.

03:15.240 --> 03:18.900
So again, try and visualize this on a much larger scale.

03:18.900 --> 03:20.910
So instead of just the small number of commits

03:20.910 --> 03:24.000
I can fit on this one slide in a tiny diagram,

03:24.000 --> 03:26.370
imagine that I'm merging pretty frequently

03:26.370 --> 03:28.350
just to get those changes from master

03:28.350 --> 03:30.000
whatever my coworkers are doing.

03:30.000 --> 03:32.610
And if it's a very, very active project

03:32.610 --> 03:35.250
that could be a lot of merging onto each feature branch.

03:35.250 --> 03:37.380
And my coworkers all have to do that too, right?

03:37.380 --> 03:38.490
I'm not the only one.

03:38.490 --> 03:39.720
They're all working on features.

03:39.720 --> 03:43.260
And if everyone else has merged changes into master

03:43.260 --> 03:45.900
they need to get those changes onto their feature branches.

03:45.900 --> 03:48.600
So everyone's feature branches might be riddled

03:48.600 --> 03:50.340
with a bunch of merge commits,

03:50.340 --> 03:53.700
and then when they merge those back into master,

03:53.700 --> 03:55.830
there's just a bunch of useless merge commits

03:55.830 --> 03:57.150
as part of the history.

03:57.150 --> 04:00.240
I shouldn't say useless, but they're not informative, right?

04:00.240 --> 04:03.900
The commits might be something like fix x bug,

04:03.900 --> 04:06.690
add new feature, remove X file,

04:06.690 --> 04:10.440
and then merge commit, merge blah branch, right?

04:10.440 --> 04:11.910
That's just sprinkled in there

04:11.910 --> 04:13.260
and that makes our history

04:13.260 --> 04:17.640
more annoying, muddier, less clean.

04:17.640 --> 04:20.760
So that is something that rebasing can help us with.

04:20.760 --> 04:23.010
So I'm gonna show an example repo,

04:23.010 --> 04:24.690
we'll set it up in the next video,

04:24.690 --> 04:27.930
but I'll just show you on slides for now,

04:27.930 --> 04:29.670
if we rebase instead.

04:29.670 --> 04:31.470
So rebasing is just another way

04:31.470 --> 04:34.860
of combining branches instead of merging.

04:34.860 --> 04:36.330
What happens when we rebase

04:36.330 --> 04:38.760
is that we actually rewrite history.

04:38.760 --> 04:41.040
And this is why it's sometimes problematic.

04:41.040 --> 04:44.010
We are rewriting history, we're creating new commits

04:44.010 --> 04:46.440
or we have git create new commits for us

04:46.440 --> 04:49.920
based upon the original feature branch commits.

04:49.920 --> 04:53.010
So here's the diagram, the same diagram from before up top.

04:53.010 --> 04:56.340
Here's my feature branch, this is what happens when I merge

04:56.340 --> 04:58.740
in all those changes right from the master branch

04:58.740 --> 05:00.900
and I get multiple merge commits.

05:00.900 --> 05:02.610
But if I use rebase,

05:02.610 --> 05:05.340
so I'm going to rebase the feature branch

05:05.340 --> 05:06.900
onto the master branch.

05:06.900 --> 05:10.290
Think of the term rebase as re...

05:10.290 --> 05:12.510
Well, that was, I was gonna say re-base.

05:12.510 --> 05:13.770
Yeah, that's exactly what it is.

05:13.770 --> 05:18.180
We are coming up with a new base for our feature branch.

05:18.180 --> 05:21.210
So what we end up with is this linear structure.

05:21.210 --> 05:24.210
We will go into detail again on how this all works

05:24.210 --> 05:27.510
and I'll demo it, but notice a linear structure,

05:27.510 --> 05:28.830
we have the master branch

05:28.830 --> 05:31.110
and then we have our feature commits

05:31.110 --> 05:34.230
from that feature branch on top of the master branch

05:34.230 --> 05:37.080
added to the tip of that master branch.

05:37.080 --> 05:39.480
So we don't have these merge commits, right?

05:39.480 --> 05:41.670
We have the same original the three commits

05:41.670 --> 05:43.080
I did on the feature branch,

05:43.080 --> 05:45.060
the four commits from the master branch,

05:45.060 --> 05:47.130
but they've actually been rewritten.

05:47.130 --> 05:50.160
So this doesn't indicate the time where things were made,

05:50.160 --> 05:51.780
where commits were created.

05:51.780 --> 05:54.030
Each commit actually has a date.

05:54.030 --> 05:57.510
So that is preserved, but commits are moved around

05:57.510 --> 05:59.339
in order to give us this new structure.

05:59.339 --> 06:01.199
So all that feature branch work I have done

06:01.199 --> 06:05.490
all those commits begin at the tip of the master branch.

06:05.490 --> 06:07.740
So I don't have these merge commits anymore,

06:07.740 --> 06:09.840
but the end result is that whatever

06:09.840 --> 06:10.890
I'm working on here

06:10.890 --> 06:14.490
contains all of the commits from master and from feature,

06:14.490 --> 06:16.440
just like it does right here.

06:16.440 --> 06:18.240
It's just a different mechanism.

06:18.240 --> 06:19.710
And here's a similar example

06:19.710 --> 06:23.610
where instead of having these merge commits, I just wait.

06:23.610 --> 06:25.110
So I have my feature branch

06:25.110 --> 06:27.660
and then I want to get the changes from master

06:27.660 --> 06:30.210
combine them with feature, those new commits,

06:30.210 --> 06:33.480
I can rebase in order to end up with this structure.

06:33.480 --> 06:36.210
My feature branch has a new base

06:36.210 --> 06:37.860
at the tip of the master branch.

06:37.860 --> 06:40.800
So it begins where master ends

06:40.800 --> 06:44.160
and we end up with this nice structure, linear, it's cleaner

06:44.160 --> 06:45.720
and it's more focused, right?

06:45.720 --> 06:48.360
The history here, someone can see very quickly

06:48.360 --> 06:50.700
these are the commits that had to do with my branch

06:50.700 --> 06:52.050
and the changes I made

06:52.050 --> 06:55.350
versus here those commits are still there,

06:55.350 --> 06:57.600
but there's these merge commits that are in the way.

06:57.600 --> 06:59.490
So if we multiply that by 10 people

06:59.490 --> 07:01.950
working on feature branches, merging all the time,

07:01.950 --> 07:03.630
that's a lot of merge commits.

07:03.630 --> 07:05.940
But if they all rebase in the correct way

07:05.940 --> 07:07.710
it's a lot easier to see what's going on

07:07.710 --> 07:09.736
with their individual branch history.

07:09.736 --> 07:11.220
All right, so I'm gonna demo this

07:11.220 --> 07:12.813
in the next couple of videos.
